# Retention (adoption and continuance)

Mechanism: continued use depends on expectation confirmation — whether real use, including verification, compliance, waiting, and coordination costs, delivers what earlier messaging promised. Every earlier overpromise comes due here. Total workflow burden decides, not time-to-first-output.

Do:
- Surface value already received ("what changed in your workflow this quarter") instead of re-running acquisition messaging.
- Acknowledge and address the supervision cost — review, verification, integration — users experience it whether or not you mention it.
- Feed retention constraints back into earlier-stage messaging: promise only what typical real use confirms.

Don't:
- Renewal pitches recycling launch superlatives.
- Celebrate first-draft speed while ignoring end-to-end completion.
- Measure retention messaging with acquisition metrics.

Example:
- ✗ "Renew now — the revolutionary platform transforming development!"
- ✓ "This quarter Acme reviewed 412 of your PRs; median review latency 9h → 3h (your dashboard). Renewing keeps your policies and history."
